# 2. Control flow

## `if`

Supported:

```python
if price > 350:
    save(p)
```

The grammar supports `if` with a suite.

AST:

```text
IfStmt
```



## `elif`

Supported:

```python
if x > 10:
    ...
elif x == 10:
    ...
```

The grammar explicitly supports zero or more `elif` clauses.

AST:

```text
ElifClause
```



## `else`

Supported:

```python
if x:
    ...
else:
    ...
```



AST:

```text
ElseClause
```



## `for`

Supported:

```python
for p in products:
    ...
```

Grammar:

```text
FOR IDENTIFIER IN expr COLON suite
```



AST:

```text
ForStmt
```

# 3. Python expressions

Your Python grammar supports these binary/logical operators:

```text
or
and
==
!=
<
>
<=
>=
+
-
*
/
%
```

and unary:

```text
not
-
```

The grammar is visible here.

## Logical OR

```python
a or b
```

## Logical AND

```python
a and b
```

## Logical NOT

```python
not a
```

## Equality

```python
a == b
```

## Not equal

```python
a != b
```

## Less than

```python
a < b
```

## Greater than

```python
a > b
```

## Less than or equal

```python
a <= b
```

## Greater than or equal

```python
a >= b
```

## Addition

```python
a + b
```

## Subtraction

```python
a - b
```

## Multiplication

```python
a * b
```

## Division

```python
a / b
```

## Modulo

```python
a % b
```

## Unary minus

```python
-x
```

The AST explicitly has:

```text
UnaryExpr
UnaryOperator
    PLUS
    MINUS
    NOT
```



Note: the current Python grammar visibly has unary `MINUS`, but not a separate unary `PLUS` grammar alternative. So `UnaryOperator.PLUS` exists in the AST infrastructure, but the current grammar does not establish `+x` as a supported Python syntax feature.

